<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In Generative Information Retrieval (GenIR), the bottleneck has shifted from generation to the selection of candidates, particularly for normative criteria such as cultural relevance. Current LLM-as-a-Judge evaluations often suffer from circularity and preference leakage, where overlapping supervision and evaluation models inflate performance. We address this by formalising cultural relevance as a within-query ranking task and introducing a leakage-free two-judge framework that strictly separates supervision (Judge B) from evaluation (Judge A). On a new benchmark of 33,052 (NGR-33k) culturally grounded stories, we find that while classical baselines yield only modest gains, a dense bi-encoder distilled from a Judge-B-supervised Cross-Encoder is highly effective. Although the Cross-Encoder provides a strong supervision signal for distillation, the distilled BGE-M3 model substantially outperforms it under leakage-free Judge A evaluation. We validate our framework on the human-curated Moral Stories dataset, showing strong alignment with human norms. Our results demonstrate that rigorous evaluator separation is a prerequisite for credible GenIR evaluation, proving that subtle cultural preferences can be distilled into efficient rankers without leakage. <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[Code and experimental settings: Anonymous] [Dataset: Anonymous]</span>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Generative Information Retrieval; Learning to Rank; LLM-as-a-Judge; Cultural Relevance; Children’s Storytelling
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative Information Retrieval (GenIR) increasingly supports systems that combine retrieval, generation, and downstream decision making, including retrieval-augmented generation and creative text applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bonifacio et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. A common operating mode is <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generate–then–select</em>: given the same constraints, a model produces multiple fluent candidates, and system quality depends primarily on <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">selection</em>—choosing the best candidate among many possible alternatives. Throughout this work, “within-query” refers to ranking candidates generated under identical constraint tuples; candidates from different queries are never compared.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Selection becomes especially difficult when the target criterion is <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normative</em> rather than purely topical. In a culturally grounded generation, candidates may be equally fluent and on-topic while differing in whether they express culturally specific settings, practices, values, or communication style. Standard lexical signals and generic dense similarity are not designed to capture such <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-topical</em> relevance dimensions, making within-query ordering unreliable under these criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>)</cite>. This setting yields candidate pools that are tightly matched in topical relevance and coherence, while differing primarily along <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normative cultural grounding</em>, a latent dimension beyond surface lexical overlap. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Mitigating Preference Leakage via Strict Estimator Separation for Normative Generative Ranking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, standard lexical rankers often struggle to reliably distinguish between surface-level stereotypes (e.g., hummus, falafel) and deep normative grounding (e.g., the specific etiquette of refusal and insisting), a gap our framework aims to bridge.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="350.4" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 350.4" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,350.4) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.5" stroke="#808080" stroke-opacity="0.5"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.54 6.79 L 3.54 336.52 C 3.54 342.22 8.17 346.85 13.88 346.85 L 470.58 346.85 C 476.29 346.85 480.92 342.22 480.92 336.52 L 480.92 6.79 C 480.92 1.08 476.29 -3.54 470.58 -3.54 L 13.88 -3.54 C 8.17 -3.54 3.54 1.08 3.54 6.79 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#464B50;" fill="#464B50" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 8.57 L 0 341.83 C 0 346.56 3.84 350.4 8.57 350.4 L 468.81 350.4 C 473.54 350.4 477.38 346.56 477.38 341.83 L 477.38 8.57 C 477.38 3.84 473.54 0 468.81 0 L 8.57 0 C 3.84 0 0 3.84 0 8.57 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 8.57 L 0.69 300.67 L 476.68 300.67 L 476.68 8.57 C 476.68 4.22 473.16 0.69 468.81 0.69 L 8.57 0.69 C 4.22 0.69 0.69 4.22 0.69 8.57 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.78 308.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.09em;--ltx-fo-height:2.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="40.47" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 37.01)" width="540.9"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.09em;">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Example:<span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Surface vs. Normative Relevance 
<br class="ltx_break">  <math id="S1.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.F1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:89%;"> Age: 10; Moral: Generosity; Culture: Arab <math id="S1.F1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="1.250em" minsize="1.250em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.78 11.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.09em;--ltx-fo-height:20.31em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="283.8" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 281.03)" width="540.9"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.09em;">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-times-circle" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#912D2D;">Candidate A (Surface):<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Ahmed sat in the <span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">desert</span> eating <span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hummus</span>… gave him a <span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">high-five</span>.”
</span> <math id="S1.F1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Stereotypical keywords, western norms.</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_inline-block fas fa-check-circle" aria-hidden="true">
</span>
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#2D557D;">Candidate B (Normative):<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Ahmed <span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">insisted</span>… guest <span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">refused three times</span>… <span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">piled more lamb</span>…”
</span> <math id="S1.F1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Captures the “ritual of refusal”.</span></span></span></span>
<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Gap:<span id="S1.F1.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Lexical rankers prefer A (keywords); Normative rankers must identify B (behavior).</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>An example of a query where dense bi-encoders and lexical models can struggle. They rank the stereotypical Candidate A higher due to token overlap, whereas the true cultural ground truth (Candidate B) requires reasoning about the enactment of the moral value.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To scale evaluation of such criteria, recent work increasingly uses large language models (LLMs) as rubric-guided judges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hashemi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. However, judge-based pipelines are vulnerable to <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">circular evaluation</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dietz et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: if the same judge (or model family) provides both supervision and evaluation, reported gains can reflect evaluator preference rather than real improvements. This risk is amplified by <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-preference bias</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, where judges systematically favour outputs from their own model family, leading to <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">preference leakage</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and inflated ranking estimates under insufficient separation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We address these challenges by casting culturally grounded story selection as a <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within-query graded ranking</em> task and introducing a <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">two-judge leakage-free framework</em>. Judge B provides rubric scores used <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> for ranking signals and training, while an independent Judge A supplies labels used <em id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> for final evaluation. To ensure fair comparison across methods, we evaluate on identical within-query candidate pools defined by the intersection of valid annotations from both judges. This design avoids missing-label confounds and ensures that all methods are evaluated on exactly the same candidate set per query. Our results show that rubric-based cultural judgments remain informative under an independent evaluator, and that this signal can be distilled <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025</a>)</cite> into neural ranking models without inference-time judging. We validate the judges’ alignment with human norms using the Moral Stories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emelin et al., 2021</a>)</cite> benchmark. Unlike prior LLM-as-a-judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> setups that conflate supervision and evaluation, our framework enforces <em id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">role separation</em> and evaluates all methods under a fixed intersection pool, enabling leakage-free, within-query ranking comparisons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Contributions:</em> We formalise culturally grounded selection as a conditional ranking optimisation problem, where the objective is to learn a scoring function <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\theta}(s\text{: story},q\text{: query})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mtext>: story</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mtext>: query</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(s\text{: story},q\text{: query})</annotation></semantics></math> that approximates a latent normative prior <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> within dense, constraint-matched candidate pools. We introduce a “strictly orthogonal two-judge” framework (<math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\rm supervision}\cap J_{\rm evaluation}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>supervision</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>evaluation</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\rm supervision}\cap J_{\rm evaluation}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>) that eliminates circular preference leakage. By enforcing evaluation exclusively on the unified intersection pool <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∩</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}</annotation></semantics></math>, we mathematically control for estimator availability bias and ensure valid within-query variance. We empirically identify a Normative Feature Suppression bottleneck in direct bi-encoder training, demonstrating that normative relevance features are latent and overshadowed by the dominant semantic components of pre-trained embeddings. We find that directly fine-tuning a bi-encoder under Judge-B normative supervision can be unstable, whereas interaction-based cross-encoders provide a strong, high-capacity teacher reference. We further show that a teacher–student distilled bi-encoder transfers much of this signal and achieves strong performance while retaining bi-encoder effectiveness. We release NGR-33k (Normative Generative Ranking dataset), a benchmark for complete reproducibility artefacts. We validate our framework’s external validity on human-curated data (Moral Stories), achieving strong inter-judge alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Retrieval vs. Selection</em>: GenIR integrates generation and retrieval and has become central to modern retrieval-augmented pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Within this space, prior work can be broadly divided into: i) <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model-based retrieval</em> that replaces explicit indexing with parametric access, and ii) <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generate-then-rank</em> pipelines that explicitly construct candidate sets and then select among them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Model-based retrieval approaches, such as Differentiable Search Indices (DSI), aim to internalise the corpus directly into model parameters, enabling retrieval via generation rather than explicit indexing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tay et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bevilacqua et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. While these approaches emphasise memorisation and fully differentiable retrieval, they are not designed for the <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within-query</em> selection problem central to our work: ordering several candidates generated under the same query constraints. They also do not engage with leakage issues that arise when rubric-based supervision and evaluation are both provided by LLM judges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, generate-then-rank pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bonifacio et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite> shift the bottleneck to <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">post-generation selection</em>, where candidates produced via stochastic decoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holtzman et al., 2020</a>)</cite> require rigorous sorting frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We explicitly target this setting by formulating culturally grounded storytelling as a <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within-query graded ranking</em> task evaluated with standard IR measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chapelle et al., 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitra and Craswell, 2018</a>)</cite>, distinguishing normative grounding from simple fluency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">LLMs as Judges and Evaluation Biases</em>: LLMs have recently been adopted as automatic evaluators for open-ended generation tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arabzadeh and Clarke, 2025</a>)</cite>, often showing strong correlation with human judgments under carefully designed rubrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hashemi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. However, judge-based evaluation is sensitive to prompting, decoding, and judge choice, and can exhibit systematic biases; this motivates uncertainty-aware reporting and framework constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yamauchi et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">lea, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A particularly concerning phenomenon is <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-preference bias</em>, where LLM judges unreasonably favour outputs generated by models from their own family or with similar training distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This bias is especially acute in within-query ranking scenarios, where candidates are highly similar and small scoring deviations can substantially alter rankings. Importantly, self-/family preference is a <em id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bias</em> of the evaluator, while leakage is a <em id="S2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">framework confound</em>: using overlapping signals for training and evaluation can make improvements appear even when they do not transfer to an independent evaluator. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite> formalise this issue as <em id="S2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">preference leakage</em>, showing that overlapping supervision and evaluation signals can lead to inflated and misleading performance estimates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Existing mitigation strategies include cross-model judging, ensemble evaluation, and prompt randomization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Our work complements these approaches by enforcing strict role separation between supervision and evaluation through a two-judge framework with blocked information flow, and by enforcing identical candidate pools via intersection filtering to remove <em id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">control</em> missing-label confounds in comparisons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees and Tice, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smucker et al., 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carterette, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Demšar, 2006</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Cultural Relevance (Non-Topical Relevance)</em>: Cultural aspects of language have been extensively studied in the context of bias detection, fairness, and harm mitigation, where culture is often treated as an attribute to control, measure, or remove <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blodgett et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Yang, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naous et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sukiennik et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Related benchmark efforts likewise implement social structure attributes primarily as risks to surface or mitigate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhamala et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bender et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Policy and governance perspectives further warn of a <em id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cultural compatibility gap</em> and <em id="S2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">algorithmic monoculture</em>, where models default toward dominant norms absent neutralising constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">While benchmarks such as CulturalBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and NormAd <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> probe factual knowledge or social acceptability, they do not address the generative selection of fully formed narratives. Our NGR-33k fills this gap by treating culture not as a bias axis, but as a positive, graded relevance signal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>)</cite> for ranking “thick” normative enactment, measurable via standard IR metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chapelle et al., 2009</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Culturally grounded children’s story generation:</em> Research on culturally grounded narratives indicates that implicit cues require treating culture as an explicit ranking objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rezapour et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rooein et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While normative resources support supervision beyond surface similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Forbes et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emelin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, rubric-based judging offers a scalable proxy for “thick evaluation” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qadri et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Unlike work framing culture as alignment diagnosis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masoud et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choenni and Shutova, 2025</a>)</cite>, we focus on explicit within-query selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Benchmark gap for culturally grounded story selection:</em> To our knowledge, no benchmark jointly supports age, moral, and culturally grounded <em id="S2.p10.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within-query ranking</em> under strict evaluator separation. While narrative datasets focus on coherence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mostafazadeh et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hill et al., 2016</a>)</cite> and cultural corpora offer broad coverage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hagedorn and Strube, 2022</a>)</cite>, none enforce this constraint triad within a fixed candidate-pool framework, motivating our controlled generate-then-rank design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Learning to Rank and Ranking Distillation</em>: Learning-to-rank (LTR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu, 2009</a>)</cite> optimizes ordering using graded signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reddy et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burges et al., 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burges, 2010</a>)</cite>. We employ ranking distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Wang, 2018</a>)</cite> to transfer expensive normative supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hashemi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> into lightweight models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou, 2018</a>)</cite>, recovering the signal without inference-time costs. However, this is only valid if improvements persist under independent evaluation, mitigating evaluator-family preference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Data and Query Construction in GenIR</em>: Several recent works construct structured or synthetic queries to study generative retrieval and ranking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> under controlled conditions, enabling reproducible evaluation and clearer attribution of ranking gains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bonifacio et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Such designs reduce topical distraction and enable analysis of <em id="S2.p12.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within-query</em> ranking behaviour, where candidates share constraints and are intentionally similar.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Our query construction follows this line of work by defining structured constraint triples (age, moral, culture) that induce candidate pools with shared topical content. This setup isolates the selection problem by ensuring that observed differences among candidates primarily reflect normative and stylistic factors rather than topical mismatch, and supports query-level evaluation using graded relevance metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chapelle et al., 2009</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">POSITIONING.</em> While GenIR identifies the post-generation selection bottleneck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bonifacio et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and LTR provides optimization mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu, 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burges et al., 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dehghani et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou, 2018</a>)</cite>, current approaches overlook a critical validity crisis: judges are plagued by instability and preference leakage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blackwell et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and culture is reduced to a bias-mitigation task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blodgett et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Yang, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">AlKhamissi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naous et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sukiennik et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We depart from this paradigm by operationalising culture as <em id="S2.p14.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded non-topical relevance</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chapelle et al., 2009</a>)</cite>. We propose the first <em id="S2.p14.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">leakage-free framework</em> using orthogonal supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and intersection pools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees and Tice, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smucker et al., 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carterette, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Demšar, 2006</a>)</cite> to rigorously disentangle true ranking gains from circular evaluator preferences.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S2.F2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:276.0pt;height:119.7pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-140.5pt,60.9pt) scale(0.495546384942451,0.495546384942451) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.F2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="334.29" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 770.67 334.29" width="770.67"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,334.29) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(68.8,0) translate(0,170.84)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#EBF5FF;" fill="#EBF5FF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 685.27 145.38 L 291.83 145.38 C 282.66 145.38 275.22 137.94 275.22 128.77 L 275.22 51.83 C 275.22 42.66 282.66 35.23 291.83 35.23 L 685.27 35.23 C 694.44 35.23 701.87 42.66 701.87 51.83 L 701.87 128.77 C 701.87 137.94 694.44 145.38 685.27 145.38 Z M 275.22 35.23"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 286.29 90.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:29.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="404.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
