)"><path d="M 6.37 0 C 5.58 0.19 2.15 1.28 0 2.46 L 0 -2.46 C 2.15 -1.28 5.58 -0.19 6.37 0 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#2D557D;" color="#2D557D" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,2.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.6pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#2D557D;--ltx-fill-color:#2D557D;" fill="#2D557D" stroke="#2D557D"><path style="fill:none" d="M 569.13 69.93 L 569.13 38.43 L 491.78 38.43 L 491.78 -58.06"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#2D557D;--ltx-fill-color:#2D557D;" fill="#2D557D" stroke="#2D557D" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 491.78 -58.06)"><path d="M 8.58 0 C 7.53 0.26 2.89 1.75 0 3.37 L 0 -3.37 C 2.89 -1.75 7.53 -0.26 8.58 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" fill="#FFFFFF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 531.56 32 h 126.4 v 12.87 h -126.4 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#2D557D;--ltx-fill-color:#2D557D;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" fill="#2D557D" stroke="#2D557D" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 534.33 34.77)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.54em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.06pt;" height="6.73" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="112.89"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.F2.pic1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#2D557D;">Parameter Transfer <math id="S2.F2.pic1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#2D557D;" mathcolor="#2D557D">θ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#2D557D;" mathcolor="#2D557D">∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#912D2D;" stroke="#912D2D" stroke-dasharray="1.6pt,2.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 291.31 -0.45 L 644.15 -0.45"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#E9D5D5;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#E9D5D5"><path d="M 574.1 5.78 L 307.97 5.78 C 304.91 5.78 302.43 3.3 302.43 0.24 L 302.43 -1.14 C 302.43 -4.2 304.91 -6.68 307.97 -6.68 L 574.1 -6.68 C 577.16 -6.68 579.64 -4.2 579.64 -1.14 L 579.64 0.24 C 579.64 3.3 577.16 5.78 574.1 5.78 Z M 302.43 -6.68"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#912D2D;--ltx-fill-color:#912D2D;" fill="#912D2D" stroke="#912D2D" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 305.2 -2.18)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:26em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:7.58pt;" height="6.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.19)" width="272.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.F2.pic1.26" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:50%;--ltx-fg-color:#912D2D;">STRICT INFORMATION BARRIER (STOP GRADIENT)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Our novel framework separates the optimisation loop (Top, Blue) from the inference/evaluation loop (Bottom, Red). The Supervision Judge (<math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>) generates noisy proxies <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> for gradient descent (<math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>). The Independent Evaluator (<math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>) provides labels <math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> exclusively for metric computation. The only bridge between the worlds is the frozen parameter set <math id="S2.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, preventing circular preference leakage. <math id="S2.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=f_{\theta^{*}}(s,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=f_{\theta^{*}}(s,q)</annotation></semantics></math> is the framework’s prediction.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Our Novel Framework</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel framework, depicted in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Mitigating Preference Leakage via Strict Estimator Separation for Normative Generative Ranking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, for culturally grounded story selection that treats cultural relevance as a <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within-query graded ranking</em> problem. Our approach addresses the “label leakage” and circular self-preference plaguing current GenIR evaluation by enforcing strict role separation between ranking supervision and independent assessment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Selection vs. Model-Based Retrieval:</em> While some GenIR directions (e.g., DSI) internalise a corpus into model parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tay et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, our work targets the <em id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">post-generation selection bottleneck</em>: ranking multiple valid candidates produced under identical constraints, common to RAG and creative generation pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Culture as Graded Non-Topical Relevance:</em> Unlike prior work that treats culture as a binary bias axis, we operationalise <em id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cultural relevance</em> as a graded, non-topical relevance signal—consistent with multidimensional relevance criteria in classical IR—enabling within-query comparisons under fixed cutoffs using standard graded metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Formal Problem Definition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure rigorous evaluation, we ground our generation constraints in established sociological and educational constructs. We define <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Age</span> as a constraint on cognitive accessibility and linguistic complexity, scaling from concrete operational stages to abstract reasoning. <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Moral Theme</span> operationalises the narrative’s pro-social intent, requiring the resolution of conflict through specific virtues (e.g., honesty, patience) rather than generic happy endings. We define <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cultural Context</span> not merely as a geographic label, but as a “thick” normative setting comprised of specific rituals, etiquette, and social dynamics that govern how the moral is enacted. Together, these three dimensions constitute the normative relevance criteria against which the generator’s alignment is measured.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We operationalise the query <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> as a triple: Target Age (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{age}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>age</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{age}}</annotation></semantics></math>) constrains complexity; Moral Theme (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{moral}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>moral</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{moral}}</annotation></semantics></math>) defines the plot resolution; and Cultural Context (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{cult}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>cult</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{cult}}</annotation></semantics></math>) provides the normative setting (e.g., Arab, Latin American), requiring grounding in customs beyond surface-level keywords.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While we treat Target Age and Moral Theme as explicit generation constraints that define the content boundary, we view Cultural Context as the primary source of normative variance. Thus, our evaluation focuses on distinguishing between surface-level thematic compliance (e.g., getting the moral right) and deep normative enactment (e.g., behaving appropriately within that moral context).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To guarantee valid outputs, we employ a rigid generation template that enforces a complete narrative arc, a strictly delimited moral resolution, and the exclusion of meta-commentary to ensure consistent parsing. The full prompt structure, which instantiates constraints such as <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(8,\text{Honesty},\text{Arab})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mtext>Honesty</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>Arab</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(8,\text{Honesty},\text{Arab})</annotation></semantics></math> into specific instruction slots, is provided in the released dataset artefacts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These three constraints constitute a minimal yet comprehensive framework that captures the essential cognitive, thematic, and normative dimensions of educational storytelling. Limiting the query to this triad isolates the core challenge of normative alignment without conflating the evaluation with purely stylistic preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Mathematical Formulation:</em> Formally, let <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of structured queries, where each <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=(c_{\text{age}},c_{\text{moral}},c_{\text{cult}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>age</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>moral</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>cult</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=(c_{\text{age}},c_{\text{moral}},c_{\text{cult}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{age}}\in\{4,\dots,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>age</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{age}}\in\{4,\dots,10\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the integer age constraint, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{moral}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>moral</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{moral}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a categorical variable from the set of morals <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{cult}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>cult</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{cult}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a categorical variable from the set of cultures <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the infinite space of possible narratives. We assume a stochastic generator <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> parameterised by <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> that defines a conditional probability distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\phi}(s|q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}(s|q)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For a given query <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a candidate set <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{q}=\{s_{1},\dots,s_{n_{q}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{q}=\{s_{1},\dots,s_{n_{q}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{i}\sim P_{\phi}(\cdot|q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\sim P_{\phi}(\cdot|q)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the size of the candidate pool <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{q}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{q}|</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume the existence of a latent target scoring function (the “ideal evaluator”) <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ordinal relevance space. In practice, <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is unobserved. We approximate it with rubric-based LLM judges under a strict two-judge framework, where Judge B provides supervision for learning, and Judge A is used only for final, leakage-free evaluation.
Our goal is to learn a ranking function <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any pair <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i},s_{j}\in\mathcal{S}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i},s_{j}\in\mathcal{S}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(s_{i},q)&gt;f_{\theta}(s_{j},q)\iff R^{*}(s_{i},q)&gt;R^{*}(s_{j},q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(s_{i},q)&gt;f_{\theta}(s_{j},q)\iff R^{*}(s_{i},q)&gt;R^{*}(s_{j},q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Two-Judge Leakage-Free Framework:</em> A central requirement is strict separation between ranking supervision and evaluation labels to prevent inflated estimates due to circular evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees and Tice, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smucker et al., 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blackwell et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laskar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We employ two rubric-guided judges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hashemi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> with non-overlapping roles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the space of ordinal relevance grades) and a generated story <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> given query <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>: <span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge B (Supervision):</span> Assigns a score <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{B}(s,q)\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{B}(s,q)\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> used <em id="S3.SS1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> for ranking supervision. <span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge A (Evaluation):</span> Assigns an independent label <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{A}(s,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{A}(s,q)</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> used <em id="S3.SS1.p9.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> for final assessment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Judge A is never used for training, early stopping, hyperparameter tuning, prompt tuning, threshold selection, or any form of model selection. Formally, we enforce strict role separation: <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is used only to train/tune <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, while all reported metrics are computed only from <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> on held-out queries. This mitigates preference leakage and self-preference bias, where evaluators disproportionately favour outputs from their own model family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We enforce this by instantiating Judge A and Judge B using different model families (e.g., Yi-34 vs. Llama-3). We employ a hierarchical rubric where Score 1 indicates irrelevant content, Score 3 indicates generic thematic alignment without cultural markers, and Score 5 requires deep integration of specific cultural customs and worldview.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Reasoning-Aware Judge Prompting:</em>
To encourage judges to evaluate the narrative substance rather than surface patterns, we employ a Chain-of-Thought (CoT) style prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barrot and Bui, 2026</a>)</cite> setup that asks the judge to consider (internally) the cultural customs and moral integration given the constraint triple <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and the rubric, and then output only a final score. Particularly, both judges receive the story text, <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and the rubric, and are instructed to reflect on cultural grounding and moral realisation before assigning a single integer score. To preserve strict output constraints and prevent extraneous text, we require JSON-only outputs and do not request or store intermediate reasoning. This reasoning-aware setup reduces the noise often seen in direct-scoring approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Unified Candidate Pool (Intersection):</em> To ensure all methods are compared on identical candidates and to avoid missing-label confounds, we define the evaluation pool per query as the intersection of valid Judge A and Judge B annotations:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}=\{s\in\mathcal{S}_{q}:y_{A}(s,q)\ \text{valid}\ \wedge\ y_{B}(s,q)\ \text{valid}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∩</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>valid</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.722em" rspace="0.722em">∧</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>valid</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}=\{s\in\mathcal{S}_{q}:y_{A}(s,q)\ \text{valid}\ \wedge\ y_{B}(s,q)\ \text{valid}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">All methods rank candidates within <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∩</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}</annotation></semantics></math>. We compute metrics only for queries with <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}|\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∩</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}|\geq k</annotation></semantics></math> at cutoff <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Järvelin and Kekäläinen, 2002</a>)</cite>.
We split data at the query level: all candidates for the same constraint tuple <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> remain in a single partition (train/dev/test) to prevent cross-query leakage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Dense Bi-Encoder:</em> We include a dense bi-encoder baseline based on BGE-M3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For each query <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we form a deterministic text serialisation <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)</annotation></semantics></math> from the constraint triple (age, moral, culture), and encode <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and each candidate story <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∩</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}</annotation></semantics></math> independently. Candidates are ranked by cosine similarity in embedding space, providing an efficient semantic baseline under the same fixed candidate pools used throughout.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We additionally report a teacher–student distilled bi-encoder (BGE-M3 Distilled). This model is implemented in SentenceTransformers and trained offline on the NGR-33k intersection training split using a cosine-similarity distillation objective, where the student bi-encoder is optimised to match a teacher-produced similarity signal derived from Judge B–supervised cross-encoder training. At inference, the distilled student encodes <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> independently and ranks by cosine similarity, yielding a lightweight dense model that transfers the teacher’s supervision while preserving bi-encoder efficiency and full locality/reproducibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">To distil the normative reasoning of the Judge-B-supervised Cross-Encoder (Teacher) into the efficient BGE-M3 Bi-Encoder (Student), we minimise the Mean Squared Error (MSE) between the teacher’s interaction-based relevance logits and the student’s cosine similarity scores. Formally, for a query <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and candidate <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, the distillation loss <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{distill}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>distill</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{distill}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{distill}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}|}\sum_{(q,s)\in\mathcal{B}}\left(\sigma(f_{\text{Teacher}}(q,s))-\cos(\mathbf{e}_{q},\mathbf{e}_{s})\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>distill</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Teacher</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{distill}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{B}|}\sum_{(q,s)\in\mathcal{B}}\left(\sigma(f_{\text{Teacher}}(q,s))-\cos(\mathbf{e}_{q},\mathbf{e}_{s})\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{Teacher}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>Teacher</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{Teacher}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the logit output of the Cross-Encoder, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid activation, and <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q},\mathbf{e}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q},\mathbf{e}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are the embeddings produced by the student. This pointwise regression allows the student to approximate the teacher’s latent normative manifold, while the bi-encoder architecture acts as a bottleneck to filter judge-specific noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Lightweight Neural Rankers over Frozen Embeddings:</em> For the supervised neural rankers (pointwise and pairwise), we freeze a dense encoder <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (BGE-M3) and learn a small scoring function on top of its representations. We define a deterministic serialisation function <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that maps the constraint tuple to a natural language string (e.g., <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q\mapsto\text{``Age: 8, Moral: Honesty...''}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mtext>“Age: 8, Moral: Honesty…”</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\mapsto\text{``Age: 8, Moral: Honesty...''}</annotation></semantics></math>). We map the query <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and candidate story <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to a joint latent representation <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}\in\mathbb{R}^{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}(s,q)=[E(T(q))\ ;\ E(s)]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">;</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}(s,q)=[E(T(q))\ ;\ E(s)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is the textual serialisation of the constraint tuple and <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\cdot;\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\cdot;\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes vector concatenation.
The scoring function <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is parameterised as a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) mapping the latent state to a scalar relevance score:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(s,q)=\mathbf{w}_{2}^{\top}\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{1}\mathbf{h}(s,q)+\mathbf{b}_{1})+b_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(s,q)=\mathbf{w}_{2}^{\top}\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{1}\mathbf{h}(s,q)+\mathbf{b}_{1})+b_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\{\mathbf{W}_{1},\mathbf{b}_{1},\mathbf{w}_{2},b_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\{\mathbf{W}_{1},\mathbf{b}_{1},\mathbf{w}_{2},b_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the learnable parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Objective 1: Pointwise Risk Minimisation:</em>
We treat the rubric scores from Judge B, denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{B}(s,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{B}(s,q)</annotation></semantics></math>, as noisy proxies for <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We frame the learning task as minimising the empirical risk under the Mean Squared Error (MSE) criterion against the discrete integer scores <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. The objective <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{(s,q)\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}}\left[\left\|f_{\theta}(s,q)-\frac{y_{B}(s,q)}{y_{\max}}\right\|^{2}_{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MSE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MSE}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{(s,q)\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}}\left[\left\|f_{\theta}(s,q)-\frac{y_{B}(s,q)}{y_{\max}}\right\|^{2}_{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p17.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\max}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\max}=5</annotation></semantics></math> is the scaling factor to normalise targets to <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Objective 2: Pairwise Probabilistic Ranking:</em>
We construct a set of preferences <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{q}=\{(i,j)\mid s_{i},s_{j}\in\mathcal{S}_{q},y_{B}(s_{i})&gt;y_{B}(s_{j})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{q}=\{(i,j)\mid s_{i},s_{j}\in\mathcal{S}_{q},y_{B}(s_{i})&gt;y_{B}(s_{j})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We model the probability of <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\succ s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\succ s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> using a logistic function of the score differences:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(s_{i}\!\succ\!s_{j}\mid\theta)=\sigma\!\big(f_{\theta}(s_{i},q)-f_{\theta}(s_{j},q)\big)=\frac{1}{1\!+\!\exp\!\left(-(f_{\theta}(s_{i},q)\!-\!f_{\theta}(s_{j},q))\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.128em">≻</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.457em;" width="0.457em"><mi mathsize="0.900em">σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.390em;" width="1.390em"><mi mathsize="0.900em">exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.072em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.072em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(s_{i}\!\succ\!s_{j}\mid\theta)=\sigma\!\big(f_{\theta}(s_{i},q)-f_{\theta}(s_{j},q)\big)=\frac{1}{1\!+\!\exp\!\left(-(f_{\theta}(s_{i},q)\!-\!f_{\theta}(s_{j},q))\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p18.2" class="ltx_p">We optimise <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> by minimising the cross-entropy loss between the Judge B preferences and the model’s induced probability distribution:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{RankNet}}(\theta)=-\sum_{q\in\mathcal{Q}}\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{P}_{q}}\log P(s_{i}\succ s_{j}|\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RankNet</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{RankNet}}(\theta)=-\sum_{q\in\mathcal{Q}}\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{P}_{q}}\log P(s_{i}\succ s_{j}|\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments and Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study GenIR in a <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generate-then-rank</em> setting, where multiple candidates are generated under identical constraints and the bottleneck becomes <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">selection</em> rather than generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alaofi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bonifacio et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manning et al., 2008</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Data and Query Construction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Story Corpus and Structured Queries:</em> We generated 35,000 children’s stories (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1. Data and Query Construction ‣ 4. Experiments and Results ‣ Mitigating Preference Leakage via Strict Estimator Separation for Normative Generative Ranking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) under explicit constraints for age, moral theme, and cultural context. After pre-processing and schema validation, we retained 33,052 stories for downstream judging and ranking. Stories are generated offline using Gemma-2-9B-IT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gemma Team, 2024</a>)</cite> with a fixed prompt template and stochastic decoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define the query space <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> as the Cartesian product of 7 ages, 7 morals, and 7 cultures, yielding <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{Q}|=343" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>343</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{Q}|=343</annotation></semantics></math> distinct constraint tuples. We targeted a corpus size of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=33{,}052" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33,052</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=33{,}052</annotation></semantics></math> to enforce dense candidate distributions (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\approx 96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\approx 96</annotation></semantics></math> per query), which are mathematically necessary to minimise estimator variance in top-<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> rank metrics, specifically at our target <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>. This high density ensures sufficient support for constructing stable pairwise preference graphs, enabling the learning of fine-grained discriminative boundaries often impossible in sparse collections. Unlike standard benchmarks where top-<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> stability is brittle, our design guarantees a robust signal within every query partition. This structure is critical for reliable variance estimation and reproducible query-level evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees and Tice, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smucker et al., 2007</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We deterministically derive a query identifier to group stories into within-query candidate sets following standard IR practice of evaluating ranked lists <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">within</em> a query context.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Dataset (<math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=33{,}052" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33,052</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=33{,}052</annotation></semantics></math>)—balanced distribution across dimensions.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:26.5pt;vertical-align:-11.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-148.8pt,11.4pt) scale(0.536958476884936,0.536958476884936) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dimension</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution (Value: Count)</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2FF;">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2FF;">Age Group</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2FF;">4 (4,713); 5 (4,895); 6 (4,568); 7 (4,773); 8 (4,412); 9 (4,947); 10 (4,744)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFF9F2;">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFF9F2;">Culture</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFF9F2;">African (4,831); Arab (4,729); E. Asian (4,628); European (4,653); Global/Neut. (4,391); Lat. Am. (5,102); S. Asian (4,718)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;">
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;">Moral Theme</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;">Bravery (4,817); Cooperation (4,828); Empathy (4,818); Honesty (4,529); Kindness (4,755); Patience (4,602); Respect (4,703)</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The intersection filtering removes missing/invalid judge outputs and ensures that every method ranks <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the same candidates</em> per query, which is critical for fair within-query comparisons under judge-based evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees and Tice, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smucker et al., 2007</a>)</cite>. On the processed NGR-33k dataset (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=33{,}052" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33,052</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=33{,}052</annotation></semantics></math>), the unified intersection pool <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{A}\cap y_{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{A}\cap y_{B})</annotation></semantics></math> retains 17,965 stories across 343 queries. Across eligible queries, intersection-pool sizes satisfy <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min/\mathrm{median}/\mathrm{mean}/\max=10/52/52.38/106" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>median</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mean</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>max</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>52</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>52.38</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>106</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min/\mathrm{median}/\mathrm{mean}/\max=10/52/52.38/106</annotation></semantics></math>, and all reported results use cutoff <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluate only queries with <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}|\geq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∩</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}|\geq k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We construct 10 query-level splits (10 seeds) so that each <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">query_id</span> and its full candidate set appear in exactly one of train/dev/test per seed. In the main setting, each seed contains 239/34/70 train/dev/test queries, i.e., 70 held-out test queries per seed. We compute metrics per query and macro-average across the 70 test queries to obtain a seed-level score, and then report mean<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>std over the 10 seeds.
Using 10 query-level splits follows standard robustness evaluation practice in IR, enabling variance estimation across random partitions without overfitting to a single test split <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voorhees and Tice, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smucker et al., 2007</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To provide an out-of-domain check, we also evaluate on the publicly available SSGEN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024d</a>)</cite> and Moral Stories datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emelin et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. We uniformly sample <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}500</annotation></semantics></math> instances with a fixed random seed (42) and score the same subset independently with Judge A and Judge B. We report each judge’s failure rate (null outputs) and inter-judge agreement on the intersection of instances with valid scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Judges and Prompts:</em> <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge A</span> (Yi-1.5-9B-Chat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">01.AI, 2024</a>)</cite>, evaluation-only) and Judge B (LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024</a>)</cite>, (supervision-only) are used to enforce strict role separation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Both use a hierarchical 1–5 rubric scoring only cultural relevance via strict JSON. Judge A includes additional anti-default guidance to prevent score compression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Decoding, Format Constraints, and Validity:</em> All judges use deterministic decoding (temperature <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0</annotation></semantics></math>, top-<math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) and output a single integer score in <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> via strict JSON formatting. A label is valid only if (i) parsing succeeds and (ii) the parsed score lies in range; otherwise, the annotation is treated as missing. Missing/invalid outputs are handled solely by intersection filtering into <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∩</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{q}^{\cap}</annotation></semantics></math> to keep candidate pools identical across methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Judge Validity and Intersection Retention:</em> On the processed NGR-33k dataset (<math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=33{,}052" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>33,052</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=33{,}052</annotation></semantics></math>), Judge A produces 8,524 null or invalid outputs (25.79%), while Judge B produces 10,912 null or invalid outputs (33.01%) under strict JSON parsing and in-range checks. Judge B is instantiated using LLaMA 3.1 8B Instruct, which exhibits conservative scoring behaviour and produces null outputs more frequently under strict JSON parsing and in-range checks. Approximately 33% of generated stories receive null supervision signals, reflecting selective supervision rather than annotation noise. All ranking models are trained and evaluated on the non-null intersection, ensuring consistent and leakage-free comparison. To ensure fair and leakage-free comparison, all methods are evaluated on the unified intersection pool <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{A}\cap y_{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{A}\cap y_{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, which retains 17,965 stories across all 343 queries. All queries remain eligible at the cutoff <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Raw generated stories and filter for valid judge outputs. <span id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2FFF2;">Intersection Pool</span> is used for evaluation.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:74.8pt;vertical-align:-34.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(2.4pt,-0.6pt) scale(1.01583331515374,1.01583331515374) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Candidate Pool Step</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Med</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mean</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="S4.T2.4.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Max</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">1. Raw Generated (<math id="S4.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{q}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{q}|</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="S4.T2.4.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">33</td>
